**<span class="mark">Gastric bypass surgery in Perth, what to expect and
how to decide</span>**

<span class="mark">If a cycle of weight loss and regain feels like a
permanent fixture in your life, you are not alone in considering weight
loss surgery as an alternative. Gastric bypass surgery is one of the
oldest, most well-known bariatric procedures in Australia, with the
Roux-en-Y gastric bypass being the most common and often the preferred
option for those managing associated health conditions such as type 2
diabetes or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) in addition to
overweight or obesity.</span>

### **<span class="mark">What is a gastric bypass?</span>**

<span class="mark">A Roux-en-Y gastric bypass involves the creation of a
small stomach pouch (roughly the size of an egg) which is then connected
directly to a lower section of the small intestine. Food travels a
shorter path (the ‘bypass’) so the patient not only feels full sooner,
the small intestine rerouting means the body absorbs fewer
calories.</span>

<span class="mark">A gastric bypass also alters your body’s hormones,
including the production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’) and blood
sugar. This is the main reason the bypass has a strong evidence base for
type 2 diabetes management. It’s not uncommon to see blood sugar
control/improvement within days of surgery, often even before
significant weight loss occurs, though results will vary based on
individual circumstances.</span>

<span class="mark">Gastric bypass in Perth has been performed
laparoscopically (via ‘keyhole surgery’) since the 1980s. The small
keyhole incisions mean less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, and
generally, a faster recovery than open surgery, though your surgeon will
discuss your goals and your different surgical options during your
initial consultation.</span>

