    nutrition and results long-term.</span>

### **<span class="mark">Benefits of gastric bypass surgery</span>**

### <span class="mark">Beyond weight loss alone, gastric bypass surgery offers a range of benefits that can improve both your physical health and day-to-day quality of life.</span>

1.  <span class="mark">Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated
    clinical average of 70-80% of excess</span>
    <span class="mark">weight over 12–18 months, though individual
    results vary.</span>

2.  <span class="mark">Strong improvement in type 2 diabetes in many
    patients, with some being able to reduce or stop medication;
    outcomes vary between individuals.</span>

3.  <span class="mark">Often relieves acid reflux and heartburn, unlike
    some procedures that can worsen it.</span>

4.  <span class="mark">Improvement in other conditions linked to
    overweight and obesity, such as high blood pressure, sleep apnoea,
    and joint pain.</span>

5.  <span class="mark">Long-term hormonal changes that reduce constant
    hunger, making healthier eating feel more achievable rather than a
    daily battle.</span>

### **<span class="mark">Risks of gastric bypass surgery</span>**

### <span class="mark">Gastric bypass surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail. </span>

1.  <span class="mark">Small risk of a leak at one of the new surgical
    connections, which are monitored closely during your hospital
    stay.</span>

2.  <span class="mark">Nutritional deficiencies (particularly vitamin
    B12, iron, and vitamin D) caused by the rerouting reduces nutrient
    absorption, meaning vitamin supplements and regular blood tests are
    recommended long-term for your health.</span>

3.  <span class="mark">Dumping syndrome; feeling unwell after eating
    sugary or fatty foods. Dumping syndrome usually improves as eating
    habits adjust.</span>

4.  <span class="mark">General surgical risks include bleeding,
    infection, blood clots, and anaesthetic reactions, as with any
    operation.</span>

**<span class="mark">How much does gastric bypass surgery cost in
Australia?</span>**

<span class="mark">Gastric bypass surgery cost in Australia varies
depending on your level of private health insurance cover, your surgeon
and anaesthetist fees, hospital and theatre charges, and any
out-of-pocket gap that applies to your situation. [<u>Medicare item
numbers 31572 and
31581</u>](https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/search.cfm?q=gastric+bypass&Submit=&sopt=S)
attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health
insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the
hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon's fee for a primary
laparoscopic gastric bypass is \$6,000 for both insured and uninsured
patients,</span> <span class="mark">rising to \$\[X,XXX\] for a complex
case. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket
costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding
option in certain circumstances, including life-threatening conditions,
certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain.
Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible
patients. A full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical
clinical fees</span> <span class="mark">can be found here.</span>

### **<span class="mark">Conversion CTA headline</span>**

<span class="mark">Ready to explore whether a gastric sleeve could
change things for you? Let's talk.  
Alt  
Ready to find out if a gastric sleeve is right for you? Book a
consultation today.</span>

### <span class="mark">FAQs (8 questions)</span>

1.  **<span class="mark">How much weight will I lose after a gastric
    bypass?</span>**

<span class="mark">On average, gastric bypass patients lose around
70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than
the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting
weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a
realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.</span>

2.  **<span class="mark">Will I get loose skin?</span>**

<span class="mark">Loose skin is common after significant weight loss.
How much loose skin your weight loss results in depends on your age,
your genetics, your skin elasticity, and of course, how much weight you
lose. Exercise, particularly resistance training, can help with body
composition during your weight loss phase. You might look into plastic
surgery for loose skin (‘body contouring’ and ‘skin removal’) as an
option down the track, though bear in mind it is not covered by the same
funding pathways.</span>

3.  **<span class="mark">How long will I need off work?</span>**

<span class="mark">Most patients require around 2-3 weeks off desk-based
or sedentary work. Physical or manual labour typically requires closer
to 6-8 weeks before returning. Everyone recovers at a different pace,
though, and your surgeon will provide guidance tailored to your
situation. It's worth factoring recovery time into your planning early,
particularly if your role involves lifting, physical exertion, or
extended periods on your feet.</span>

4.  **<span class="mark">What if it doesn't work for me?</span>**

<span class="mark">A gastric bypass is one of the most effective
bariatric procedures available, but results depend on a combination of
surgery and sustained lifestyle change. If weight loss is slower than
expected or regain occurs over time, the first step is reviewing diet,
habits, and follow-up care with your team. Further surgical options may
be considered in some cases. Your surgeon and dietitian are there to
support you through challenges, not just the initial recovery.</span>

5.  **<span class="mark">Can I eat normally/what can I never eat after a
    gastric bypass?</span>**

<span class="mark">The great news is that most foods can be reintroduced
over time. But ‘normal’ does look different after a bypass. Portions
will be much smaller, eating too quickly (or too much) can cause
discomfort, and some patients experience dumping syndrome — a reaction
to high-sugar/high-fat foods that can cause nausea, cramping, and
lightheadedness. Most people adapt well though and many find their
relationship with food improves meaningfully over time.</span>

6.  **<span class="mark">Is gastric bypass better than a gastric
    sleeve?</span>**

<span class="mark">Neither procedure is universally better. The bypass
tends to produce slightly greater weight loss and has stronger outcomes
for type 2 diabetes and acid reflux, but it is more complex with greater
nutritional demands long-term. The sleeve is less invasive and leaves
your digestion intact. The right choice is an individual one, best made
in consultation with your surgeon after a thorough assessment of your
health history and goals.</span>

7.  **<span class="mark">Do I need a GP referral?</span>**

<span class="mark">A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book
a consultation, but having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates
to apply to your specialist consultation fee, reducing your
out-of-pocket cost for the appointment. It also ensures your surgeon has
your relevant medical history from the outset. If you don't yet have a
referral, our team can advise you on next steps.</span>

8.  **<span class="mark">Where is the surgery performed?</span>**

<span class="mark">The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric
bypass surgery in Perth at three locations: [<u>Mount Private
Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private
Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [<u>Waikiki Private
Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Pre- and post-operative
(once you’ve been discharged from hospital) consultations take place at
Garden City Specialist Centre, Booragoon, and WA Cardiology Centre in
Greenfields.</span>

