    54	### **<span class="mark">What is a gastric bypass?</span>**
    55	
    56	<span class="mark">A Roux-en-Y gastric bypass involves the creation of a
    57	small stomach pouch (roughly the size of an egg) which is then connected
    58	directly to a lower section of the small intestine. Food travels a
    59	shorter path (the ‘bypass’) so the patient not only feels full sooner,
    60	the small intestine rerouting means the body absorbs fewer
    61	calories.</span>
    62	
    63	<span class="mark">A gastric bypass also alters your body’s hormones,
    64	including the production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’) and blood
    65	sugar. This is the main reason the bypass has a strong evidence base for
    66	type 2 diabetes management. It’s not uncommon to see blood sugar
    67	control/improvement within days of surgery, often even before
    68	significant weight loss occurs, though results will vary based on
    69	individual circumstances.</span>
    70	
    71	<span class="mark">Gastric bypass in Perth has been performed
    72	laparoscopically (via ‘keyhole surgery’) since the 1980s. The small
    73	keyhole incisions mean less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, and
    74	generally, a faster recovery than open surgery, though your surgeon will
    75	discuss your goals and your different surgical options during your
    76	initial consultation.</span>
