    82	### What is a gastric bypass?
    83	- ~3 paragraphs
    84	- **Para 1:** Define the Roux-en-Y procedure in plain terms. The surgeon creates a small stomach pouch (roughly the size of an egg) and connects it directly to a lower section of the small intestine. Food travels a shorter path, so the patient feels full sooner and the body absorbs fewer kilojoules.
    85	- **Para 2:** Explain the dual mechanism — volume restriction (smaller pouch) and hormonal change (gut hormones controlling hunger, fullness and blood sugar are altered by the rerouting). This is the reason the bypass has a strong evidence base for type 2 diabetes management. Many patients see blood sugar improvement within days of surgery, often before significant weight loss occurs — qualify clearly that the extent and duration of improvement varies between individuals.
    86	- **Para 3:** Note that the operation is performed keyhole (laparoscopic) through small incisions — less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, quicker recovery than open surgery. Add that the surgeon confirms keyhole suitability at consultation.
    87	
    88	**Comparison note to weave in:** Compared to a gastric sleeve, the bypass tends to give slightly more weight loss and stronger results for type 2 diabetes and acid reflux, but it is a more involved operation with lifelong vitamin needs. The right choice is made with the surgeon based on the individual's health and goals.
    89	
